PURPOSE
The intent behind this lab is to launch a real attack (on my own VM) and see if I can retrace what happened via Wazuh logs. The best way to learn is by doing, so I want to see what I can learn.

ARMITAGE INSTALL 
First, I had to get Armitage running. All of this is possible to do through the Metasploit via CLI (I learned this in a recent SANS course), but Armitage GUI makes it easier. I needed to install Armitage, and after doing so, I launched Armitage. It asked which host I would like to run it against, and interestingly, Ubuntu refused the connection. I verified that the two machines can actuall talk to each other (via ping), so there were no network issues.

VERIFICATION IN WAZUH
However, what I can see in Wazuh so far is that Armitage was installed. Right there at the top, a new medium alert came in showing:
data.package: armitage
With a status of installed. This means that Wazuh is indeed detecting that new things are being installed, and the rule level was 7. According to Wazuh's documentation, this means that a keyword was triggered and likely signifies a security issue.

NMAP SCAN
Knowing that Ubuntu can reach Kali and vice versa, and that Wazuh knows something suspicious may be incoming, I decided to run an Nmap scan on the Ubuntu machine. This would be good to tell me which ports are available to potentially use Armitage on, since the default value did not work. While that didn't trigger any directly correlated alerts, it did send a level 3 alert that sudo was used. Wazuh states that a level 3 alert shows successful events, such as successful logins or firewall allows.

SECOND ATTEMPT AT ARMITAGE & INTERESTING ALERT IN WAZUH
This time, using a port that Nmap identified, I was able to get Armitage to connect. I temporarily disabled the firewall on Ubuntu to make it more intentionally vulnerable. Wazuh did also catch that, but only at level 3. It did show the command used and tell me that root was logged into (sudo used), the firewall disable was run, and then the connection was closed. While waiting for Armitage to fully open the connection, a log came into Wazuh that seemed interesting. It showed netstat listening ports on Ubuntu. I never ran such a command, but further investigation determined that this is automatic process by Wazuh when those listening ports change. It does show as a level 7 alert.

ARMITAGE SCAN TROUBLESHOOTING
Once Armitage was up and I had gotten the Ubuntu host imported, I scanned the host. This performs another Nmap scan to verify what ports are open and what Metasploit modules could potentially be run on the target. I then attempted to launch a Hail Mary attack, which is a mass-attack to exploit almost anything it can find. However, nothing happened, and I couldn't get it to show specific attack options. My first thought was that updating/upgrading Kali might find exploits I could use. Wazuh also logged that as newpackages being installed.
Finally, after stopping and restarting the services, Armitage reconnected to the host and was able to query potential exploits.

ARMITAGE ATTACK
I launced the exploit/linux/http/accelion_fta_getstatus_oath exploit with the shell_bind_tcp payload, which was successful. This opened up a shell on the target machine for free use. Whoami was run to start, to figure out what user I was currently logged in as on the target machine. I was also able to run "cat /etc/passwd" which, while password hashes are not dumped here, can still give valuable information into the environment. /etc/passwd can show what users exist and what primary group they belong to, as well potential other information depending on user settings. Next, I tried to run "cat /etc/shadow", which is where the password hashes ARE stored. I took a guess at the user's password from the whoami output, and I was correct (from a hacker perspective, anyway. Of course I knew what the password was, and I made it intentionally vulnerable). The full command that gave access to the file was "echo "[password]" | sudo -S cat /etc/shadow". And now I have user password hashes at my disposal. I was also able to run a command (passwd -d) to delete the password for that account with my newfound access. Finally, I was able to run "cat /etc/*release" to gather information on the system itself, such as the fact that the system runs Kali and which Kali version being used.

FINDING THE ATTACK IN WAZUH
I was able to find the commands I ran in Wazuh. Wazuh does alert on all of them (filter by data.command); however, they are showing as level 3 alerts, with the exception of the new user add (which was level 8). After further investigation, it looks like what caused the alert was that it was a first time user running those commands, rather than the commands themselves. In that context, I agree with the severity rating. It also correctly identifies these commands as Privilege Escalation and Defense Evasion. I can also view surrounding documents, essentially giving context to what happened.

CONCLUSION
Wazuh was able to successfully allow me to reconstruct what happened during this simulated attack. It alerted on several stages: metasploit/armitage installation (which would only be useful for an insider threat, as it only picked this up because they were on the same network and Kali had a Wazuh agent installed), firewall changes, privileged command execution, account creation, and password deletion. Wazuh did classify many, if not all, of these as low severity alerts, but given the chance to investigate those lower severity logs, reconstruction of the attack stages is possible.
This lab not only highlighted the importance of a SIEM in general to alert on potentially suspicious activity, but also the importance of fine tuning what alerts are generated and at what severity level. It also gave me unique hands-on practice with discovering and following an attack step-by-step.
